  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled and running with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds:** The `kibana_sample_data_logs` index has about 14,000 web requests, timestamped from Oct 4 to Dec 3, 2026, so most of them are dated in the future. Each request records:\n- **Response codes:** 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441). 503 is the only 5xx code, and those requests all have 0 bytes.\n- **Other fields:** bytes, client IP (1,000 distinct), source and destination country, OS, file extension, URL, referrer, user agent and host.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-84b75a4d Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-84b75a4d-web-logs-overview-df1435bbbccf\n- Number tiles for total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes\n- Top 10 source countries\n\nThe default time range runs from 7 days ago to 60 days ahead, because most of the sample data is dated after today.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-84b75a4d 5xx spike\"** (id `c5c40157-ac37-48f8-89b3-d5602d497921`) runs every minute. It counts requests in `kibana_sample_data_logs` with a response code from 500 to 599, using the `timestamp` field. It fires when more than 10 of them arrive within 5 minutes. I checked that this 5xx filter matches all 441 of the 503 requests.\n\nThe rule has no action attached, so when it fires it only records an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone. If you want Slack, email or log notifications, a connector needs to be added.",
